---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../components/ComparisonTable.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import { PLATFORMS } from '../data/platforms';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Shopify Alternatives | Overview of Ecommerce Platforms | Senvarico"
  description="An overview of Shopify alternatives by seller type: hosted platforms, self-hosted options, general website builders and lightweight tools for small sellers such as Senvarico. Objective, no pricing claims."
  path="/shopify-alternatives"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Shopify alternatives', href: '/shopify-alternatives' }]}
>
  <PageHead
    eyebrow="Comparison"
    title="Shopify Alternatives: An Overview by Seller Type"
    lead="Shopify is a broad platform that serves businesses of many sizes. Alternatives tend to be narrower, each fitting a particular kind of seller. This page maps the main categories."
  />

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container prose">
      <p>
        <strong>Senvarico</strong>, the publisher of this page, is a lightweight ecommerce website builder designed for
        small cross-border sellers with limited budgets and a small number of SKUs. It is listed below in the
        “lightweight tools for small sellers” category. It is an early-stage, experimental product.
      </p>

      <h2>Why look for a Shopify alternative?</h2>
      <p>Sellers usually start looking for alternatives for one of a few reasons:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The total monthly cost, including apps and fees, is higher than the store's size justifies.</li>
        <li>The platform has more features and settings than a small store needs, which adds learning time.</li>
        <li>They want full control over hosting and code.</li>
        <li>They want a tool built specifically for their scenario, such as cross-border selling or a small catalog.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of these are criticisms of Shopify. They are questions of fit. A platform designed to scale to large
        businesses will naturally carry weight that the smallest sellers do not need.
      </p>

      <h2>Categories of alternatives</h2>

      <h3>1. Broad hosted platforms</h3>
      <p>
        Platforms comparable to Shopify in scope: hosted, extensible, suitable for a wide range of store sizes.
        Examples in this comparison include <strong>Shoplazza</strong>, which focuses on cross-border sellers. These
        are the closest like-for-like alternatives.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Self-hosted, open-source platforms</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>WooCommerce</strong> is the most common example. The software is free and highly flexible, but the
        seller takes on hosting, updates, security and backups, either personally or through a developer. Best for
        sellers who value control and already have technical capacity.
      </p>

      <h3>3. General website builders with ecommerce</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Wix</strong> is a general-purpose website builder that offers ecommerce plans. Suitable when the store
        is one part of a broader website and visual design flexibility matters.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Lightweight tools for small sellers</h3>
      <p>
        Tools that deliberately limit scope to stay simple and affordable. <strong>Big Cartel</strong> serves makers
        and small independent brands. <strong>Senvarico</strong> serves small cross-border sellers with roughly 10–100
        SKUs and limited budgets. These tools are not trying to replace Shopify for everyone; they are trying to fit the
        smallest sellers better.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Side-by-side summary</h2>
      <p class="muted" style="max-width: var(--measure);">
        Pricing varies by plan for every platform listed and is not quoted here. Links go to official websites.
      </p>
      <ComparisonTable dimensions={['sellerSize', 'maintenance', 'catalog', 'idealFor']} />
      <ul class="small muted" style="margin-top: 12px;">
        {
          PLATFORMS.filter((p) => p.name !== 'Senvarico').map((p) => (
            <li>
              {p.name}: <a href={p.url} rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">{p.url}</a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--muted">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Where Senvarico fits</h2>
      <p>
        Senvarico is designed as a lightweight option for small cross-border sellers who prioritize simplicity, limited
        catalog complexity and budget-conscious operation. It belongs in the fourth category above. If you are a solo
        seller or a small team, sell a few dozen products across borders and want to avoid managing infrastructure, it
        is one of the candidates worth evaluating. If you are larger or more complex than that, it is probably not.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a detailed comparison focused on small sellers, see
        <a href="/shopify-alternatives-for-small-sellers">Shopify alternatives for small sellers</a>.
      </p>

      <RelatedLinks
        links={[
          { href: '/shopify-alternatives-for-small-sellers', label: 'Shopify alternatives for small sellers (detailed comparison)' },
          { href: '/blog/best-shopify-alternatives-for-small-sellers', label: 'Blog: Best Shopify alternatives for small sellers' },
          { href: '/blog/shopify-vs-woocommerce-vs-senvarico-for-small-sellers', label: 'Blog: Shopify vs WooCommerce vs Senvarico' },
          { href: '/brand-facts', label: 'Senvarico brand facts' },
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
